A = {
330: "Do a level-order (breadth-first) traversal with a queue, processing one level at a time, and keep a flag for the direction: collect each level's values in a list and reverse it on every other level before printing. A two-stack version avoids reversing: push children left-then-right for one direction and right-then-left for the other. Time O(n), space O(width of the tree).",
331: "An initial block runs once, starting at time 0, and is used in testbenches for stimulus and setup (and on FPGAs for initial register values); synthesis otherwise ignores it. An always block runs repeatedly forever, re-triggered by its sensitivity list or timing controls; always @(posedge clk) describes flip-flops and always @(*) combinational logic, so it is the synthesizable construct for hardware.",
332: "Keep the type of the previous command in the sequence and constrain the next one: in body(), loop 10 times; create the item; start_item(); randomize with { if (prev_was_write) cmd != READ; }; finish_item(); then set prev_was_write = (item.cmd == WRITE). The first item is unconstrained (it may be a write). Alternatively randomize an array of 10 commands at once with constraint foreach (c[i]) if (i > 0 && c[i-1] == WRITE) c[i] != READ;.",
333: "Packed arrays (bit [7:0] a) are a contiguous vector that can be used as a number. Unpacked fixed-size arrays (int a[8]) hold separate elements. Dynamic arrays (int a[]) are sized at run time with new[]. Associative arrays (int a[string] or a[int]) are sparse and indexed by any key, good for memory models. Queues (int q[$]) grow and shrink at both ends with push_back, pop_front and insert. They come with methods such as size, delete, sort, find and sum.",
334: "An inverter can be built from a NAND gate with its inputs tied together (or one input tied to 1), from a NOR gate with inputs tied together (or one input tied to 0), from an XOR gate with one input tied to 1, from a 2:1 MUX with d0 = 1, d1 = 0 and the signal on the select, or from a decoder output. The point is that any universal gate can make NOT.",
335: "Size the PMOS wider than the NMOS to balance rise and fall delays, because hole mobility is about 2 to 3 times lower than electron mobility: typically Wp/Wn = 2 to 3 for equal drive and a switching threshold near VDD/2. Then scale both up together for the load: a larger inverter drives more capacitance faster but presents more input capacitance to the previous stage, so chains are sized with a constant fan-out of about 4 per stage (logical effort).",
336: "Near-zero static power (only leakage, because no DC path exists in steady state), full rail-to-rail output swing, high noise margins, very high input impedance, inverting gates built from complementary pull-up and pull-down networks, delay set by charging load capacitance, and excellent scaling and density. Its main power cost is dynamic switching power, plus leakage in advanced nodes.",
337: "CMOS gates draw almost no static current, give full logic levels and good noise margins, have very high input impedance so one gate can drive many others, and scale well to billions of transistors on a chip. The complementary networks make logic design regular (series and parallel transistors map to AND and OR), and the process is cheap and mature.",
338: "Pipeline design splits a long path into stages separated by registers so that a new operation can start every cycle; throughput rises and the clock gets faster, at the cost of latency and registers. Hazards are cases where the next instruction cannot proceed: structural (resource conflicts), data (dependencies on results not ready yet, fixed by forwarding and stalls) and control (branches, fixed by prediction and flushing). Balancing stage delays and handling hazards are the main design tasks.",
339: "Pipelining breaks a task into stages and overlaps them, like an assembly line: while one item is in stage 3, the next is in stage 2 and the one after in stage 1. Each stage has its own register, so after the pipeline fills one result comes out every clock. It increases throughput and allows a higher clock frequency; latency per item increases slightly, and dependencies between items cause hazards.",
340: "The clock period must cover the longest combinational path between registers. Inserting pipeline registers cuts a long path into shorter ones, so the worst stage delay, and therefore the minimum period, gets smaller: ideally a path of delay T split into k balanced stages allows a period of about T/k plus register overhead (clock-to-Q, setup and skew). The overhead limits the gain from very deep pipelines.",
341: "Instruction Fetch (read the instruction at PC from instruction memory and compute PC + 4), Instruction Decode (decode, read the register file, sign-extend immediates, detect hazards), Execute (ALU operation, address calculation, branch comparison), Memory access (load or store data memory), and Write Back (write the result to the register file).",
342: "Read the netlist and libraries, define the test protocol (scan clocks, resets, scan enable, test mode), and run DFT design rule checks to find uncontrollable clocks and resets, latches and loops. Then replace flops with scan flops, decide the number and length of chains (balanced, per clock domain and edge), stitch the chains with lockup latches where domains or edges mix, connect scan-in, scan-out and scan-enable ports (possibly with compression logic), re-run DRC, and write out the scan netlist, the scan DEF for chain reordering in placement, and the test protocol for ATPG.",
343: "Wire delay grows with the square of length (distributed RC), so the 100 um wire's 100 us scales as L^2. Segments of 20, 30 and 50 um give 100 x (0.2^2 + 0.3^2 + 0.5^2) = 100 x 0.38 = 38 us of wire delay, plus 2 buffers x 30 us = 60 us, so about 98 us in total: slightly better than 100 us. (Equal segments would do better; with a linear wire model the buffers would only add delay.)",
344: "Read the diagram cycle by cycle: list the output value in each clock cycle against the inputs, and decide whether the output depends only on the current inputs (combinational), on past values (flops or counters), or on a sequence (an FSM). Write a state table, choose the state encoding, derive the next-state and output equations with K-maps, and check the result by drawing its waveform against the given one, including the reset behaviour.",
345: "Use a Moore or Mealy FSM. Mealy with two states: S0 (last bit was 0 or reset) and S1 (last bit was 1); in S1, an input of 1 outputs 1 and stays in S1 (overlapping, so 111 gives two outputs), and a 0 goes back to S0. Equivalently, register the previous bit and output in & prev. If the pairs must not overlap, go from S1 to S0 after a match instead.",
346: "Packed dimensions are declared before the name (bit [7:0][3:0] p) and are stored contiguously as one vector, so the whole array can be used as a number, sliced, and assigned with arithmetic; only bit-level types can be packed. Unpacked dimensions come after the name (int u [4][8]) and are separate elements, which can be of any type, are accessed element by element, and are how memories are modelled. You can mix them: logic [7:0] mem [0:255] is 256 unpacked bytes.",
347: "ATPG automatically generates test patterns for a fault model, usually stuck-at and transition faults on a scan design. For each fault it activates the fault (sets the faulty node to the opposite value), propagates the effect to a scan flop or output, and justifies the required values back to controllable inputs (D-algorithm, PODEM, FAN). Fault simulation then drops all other faults the pattern detects, and pattern compaction merges patterns. Results are reported as fault coverage and test coverage; untestable and redundant faults are classified separately.",
348: "For each fault, first activate it: set the faulty line to the opposite of its stuck value. Then sensitise a path to an output: set every side input of gates along the path to its non-controlling value (1 for AND and NAND, 0 for OR and NOR). Finally justify all those values back to the primary inputs. Write the resulting input vector and verify that the good and faulty circuits give different outputs; one vector often detects several faults.",
349: "A programmable delay line built from a chain of delay cells with multiplexers that select a tap. Typical errors: glitches when the select changes while the signal is high (change the select only when the input is stable, or resynchronise it), unequal mux delays making the steps non-monotonic, an inverted or reversed select ordering, missing buffering between taps, and not accounting for PVT variation of the delay cells. Check monotonicity and glitch-free switching.",
350: "A pulse detector usually samples a signal with flops and compares current and delayed values. It fails when the pulse is shorter than a clock period (it can fall between two edges and be missed), when the input is asynchronous and not synchronised (metastability), when two pulses come closer than the detector's recovery time, at reset or power-up when the delayed copy is X, and when glitches create false detections. The fix is to synchronise, stretch short pulses or use a toggle-based crossing.",
351: "Compare the waveforms cycle by cycle and find the relationship: a delay (flops), a frequency division (a counter or toggle flop), an edge-triggered pulse (a flop and an AND or XOR with the delayed copy), a duty-cycle change (counting on both edges), or a combination. Then build the minimum logic and check that the reset value and the phase relative to the clock match.",
352: "Treat capacitors as open at low frequency and as short at high frequency (inductors the reverse), then see which path dominates. For an RC network that means finding whether the output is taken across C (low-pass: passes DC, rolls off above 1/(2 pi R C)) or across R (high-pass: blocks DC, passes high frequencies). Around the corner frequency the gain is -3 dB and the phase 45 degrees; beyond it, the gain falls at 20 dB per decade per pole.",
353: "A wire is a net: it has no storage and must be continuously driven, by an assign statement or a module output. A reg is a variable that holds the last value assigned to it in an always or initial block. Despite its name, a reg does not have to be a register: in always @(*) it becomes combinational logic. SystemVerilog replaces both in most code with logic, which can be driven either way (by a single driver).",
354: "Maximise parallelism and memory efficiency: enough threads to fill the GPU (occupancy), coalesced global memory accesses so a warp reads contiguous addresses, tiling to reuse data in shared memory or registers, avoiding shared-memory bank conflicts, minimising branch divergence within a warp, using tensor cores and lower precision where possible, overlapping data transfers with compute, fusing kernels to reduce memory round-trips, and checking with a profiler whether the kernel is memory- or compute-bound.",
355: "Retiming moves registers across combinational logic to balance stage delays without changing behaviour. A script would read the netlist and timing report, find stages whose slack is unbalanced, and move registers backward across a gate (one register on each input replaced by one on the output) or forward, keeping the number of registers on every cycle in the circuit the same; then rerun timing. In practice you enable the synthesis tool's retiming option and use the script to choose which modules or paths it may touch.",
356: "Without a multiply instruction, use shift and add: clear the result; loop while the multiplier is not zero: if its lowest bit is 1, add the multiplicand to the result; shift the multiplicand left and the multiplier right. With a multiply instruction it is one line (MUL r3, r1, r2 on Arm or RISC-V). Mention overflow (the product needs twice the width) and the signed case.",
357: "Fuses (eFuses or anti-fuses) are one-time-programmable bits blown at test time or in the field. They store chip-specific data that must survive power-off: memory repair information (which redundant rows or columns replace failing ones), trimming values for analog blocks and PLLs, unique IDs and calibration, secure boot keys and security settings, and feature or SKU configuration. They are read by a fuse controller at boot.",
358: "APB is the simple, low-power peripheral bus: no pipelining, a two-phase transfer (setup with PSEL, then access with PENABLE), an optional PREADY for wait states and PSLVERR for errors, used for register access. AHB is a higher-performance bus: pipelined address and data phases, bursts (incrementing and wrapping), wider data, a single master per transfer with arbitration in multi-master systems, HREADY for wait states and HRESP for errors. AXI goes further with separate channels and outstanding transactions.",
359: "Use a modified binary search for the rotation point: compare a[mid] with a[hi]; if a[mid] > a[hi], the drop (and the maximum just before it) is in the right half, otherwise in the left. The maximum is the element just before the minimum (or the last element if the array is not rotated). This is O(log n); with duplicates it can degrade to O(n).",
360: "Find the longest register-to-register path. fmax = 1 / (Tclk-to-Q + Tcomb,max + Tsetup + Tskew_effect + uncertainty), where a capture clock that arrives later than the launch clock helps setup. Also check hold separately (Tclk-to-Q + Tcomb,min >= Thold + skew), because hold does not depend on frequency.",
361: "PCIe 6.0 moved to PAM4 signalling with FLIT (flow control unit) mode, adding forward error correction and a stronger CRC, and it extended the TLP header format to carry more information (for example additional fields and prefixes for new features). These additions improve reliability at 64 GT/s and support new capabilities, at the cost of a few more header and protection bytes per transfer.",
362: "A function executes in zero time and cannot block, returns a value (or is void), and can be called from expressions and constraints; it may only call functions. A task can block and consume time (delays, waits, event controls, calls to other tasks) and returns values through output or ref arguments. In classes, time-consuming methods such as driving a bus must be tasks.",
363: "Name the circuit first by its structure: trace the inputs through the gates or flops, write the Boolean equations or the next-state equations, and recognise the function (for example an adder, a mux, a counter or an edge detector). Then write the Verilog in the clearest style: assign statements for combinational logic and an always_ff block for registers, with the same reset behaviour as the circuit, and verify with a small testbench.",
364: "Performance: pipeline long paths, balance stages, avoid deep priority logic, use one-hot FSMs, and register outputs. Power: gate clocks with enables, avoid unnecessary toggling (operand isolation, hold data when not valid), and reduce memory accesses. Area: share resources such as adders and multipliers across cycles, avoid redundant logic and over-wide signals, prefer RAMs over flop arrays, and keep reset only where needed. Every choice trades one against another.",
365: "Divide the board clock (for example 50 MHz) with a counter to make a 1 Hz enable; cascade counters for seconds (0 to 59), minutes (0 to 59) and hours (0 to 23), each incrementing when the previous rolls over; convert each digit to BCD and then to seven-segment codes, and multiplex the displays with a faster refresh enable (around 1 kHz). Add debounced buttons to set the time, use clock enables instead of generated clocks, and constrain the main clock.",
366: "RTL (verified) is synthesised with timing constraints into a gate-level netlist, scan and DFT are inserted, and equivalence is checked. In physical design: floorplan (die size, macros, IO, power grid), placement and optimisation, clock tree synthesis, routing and post-route optimisation. Then extraction of parasitics and sign-off: STA across corners, IR drop and EM, crosstalk, DRC, LVS, antenna and density checks, with ECOs to fix issues, and finally the GDSII file is streamed out for tape-out.",
367: "An internally compensated op-amp has a very high DC gain (100 dB or more) that stays flat up to a low dominant pole (often a few Hz to tens of Hz), then falls at -20 dB per decade, crossing 0 dB at the unity-gain bandwidth (the gain-bandwidth product, for example 1 MHz). The phase drops to -90 degrees after the dominant pole; a second pole near or beyond unity gain sets the phase margin. Closed-loop gain G gives a bandwidth of about GBW / G.",
368: "A PMOS connects the output to VDD and an NMOS connects it to ground, with both gates on the input. Input low: the PMOS is on and the NMOS off, so the output is pulled to VDD. Input high: the NMOS is on and the PMOS off, so the output goes to 0. Only during the transition do both conduct briefly. In steady state no current flows, so static power is almost zero and the output swings rail to rail.",
369: "As the input rises: (1) NMOS off, PMOS linear, output high; (2) NMOS saturated, PMOS linear, output starts falling; (3) both saturated around the switching threshold, highest gain; (4) NMOS linear, PMOS saturated; (5) PMOS off, NMOS linear, output low. The steep middle region is where the inverter amplifies and where noise margins are defined.",
370: "The scope's and probe's bandwidth should be about 3 to 5 times the highest frequency of interest so its own response does not distort the signal. For digital signals, the edges matter more than the clock frequency: use the rise time, BW = 0.35 / t_rise (for example a 350 ps edge needs at least 1 GHz, ideally more). Match the probe bandwidth and loading too.",
371: "A demultiplexer routes one input to one of several outputs selected by the select lines: with n select bits it has 2^n outputs, and the unselected outputs are 0. It is a decoder with a data input used as the enable. Uses: distributing data to one of several destinations, address decoding (enabling one memory chip or register), and serial-to-parallel conversion.",
372: "RAM (random access memory) can be read and written during normal operation and is usually volatile, losing its contents without power; it holds working data. ROM (read-only memory) holds fixed contents written at manufacture or programming time and is non-volatile; it holds boot code and firmware. Programmable variants (PROM, EPROM, EEPROM, flash) blur the line but are still mainly read.",
373: "A mutex is a lock with ownership: only the thread that locked it can unlock it, and it protects one critical section. A semaphore is a counter: wait or get decrements it and blocks at zero, signal or put increments it, and any thread can signal; a counting semaphore controls access to N identical resources and can also be used for signalling between threads. A binary semaphore behaves like a mutex without ownership.",
374: "Both put an Arm core into a low-power standby state. WFI (wait for interrupt) wakes up on an interrupt or a debug event. WFE (wait for event) wakes up on an event, which can be an interrupt but also an SEV (send event) instruction from another core or the event register already being set; it is typically used in spinlocks, so a core waiting for a lock sleeps until the owner signals the release with SEV.",
375: "With a temporary: t = a; a = b; b = t;. Without a temporary, using XOR: a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b; (fails if a and b are the same variable or memory location), or with arithmetic: a = a + b; b = a - b; a = a - b; (can overflow). In Verilog, a non-blocking swap works directly: a <= b; b <= a;.",
376: "SPI uses four wires (SCLK, MOSI, MISO and one chip select per slave), is full duplex, has no addressing or acknowledgement, and runs at tens of MHz. I2C uses two open-drain wires (SCL, SDA) with pull-ups, has 7- or 10-bit addressing, ACK and NACK, multiple masters with arbitration, and slower speeds (100 kHz, 400 kHz, 1 MHz, 3.4 MHz). Clock stretching exists only in I2C: a slave holds SCL low to make the master wait. Test cases: all SPI modes (CPOL and CPHA), chip-select timing, back-to-back transfers; for I2C, address match and mismatch, ACK and NACK, repeated start, clock stretching, arbitration loss, and bus timing at each speed.",
377: "Each line (SDA and SCL) has one pull-up resistor to VDD, because devices only drive the lines low. The value is a trade-off: small enough that the RC rise time with the bus capacitance meets the spec (for example 1000 ns at 100 kHz, 300 ns at 400 kHz), so Rmax = t_r / (0.8473 Cbus); large enough that a device sinking 3 mA can pull the line below VOL, so Rmin = (VDD - VOL) / IOL. Typical values are 2.2 k to 10 kohm.",
378: "Setup time is the minimum time the D input must be stable before the active clock edge, so the flip-flop can capture it reliably. Internally the master latch needs time for the data to propagate through its input stage before it closes. If data changes inside the setup window, the output may take the wrong value or go metastable. In STA the setup check requires data arrival plus setup time to be no later than the capture clock edge.",
379: "Reuse the block-level environments: each block's agents become passive monitors on internal interfaces, while active agents drive only the IP's external ports. Integrate the block scoreboards and add an end-to-end scoreboard for the full data path, use a virtual sequencer to coordinate traffic across interfaces, use the RAL model for register configuration, and focus the tests on interactions between blocks, shared resources, clocks and resets, and system scenarios, with coverage across blocks.",
380: "Analysis ports broadcast to any number of subscribers through write(). Alternatives: put and get ports (blocking or non-blocking) for point-to-point transfer between two components, a uvm_tlm_fifo or uvm_tlm_analysis_fifo between them to buffer, analysis imps with the `uvm_analysis_imp_decl macro when a component needs several analysis inputs, transport ports for request and response, or, outside TLM, callbacks, events and a shared object handle.",
381: "A virtual sequence runs on a virtual sequencer that holds handles to several real sequencers, so it can coordinate stimulus across multiple interfaces or agents (for example configure through APB, then start traffic on AXI, then wait for an interrupt). A virtual interface is a handle to a SystemVerilog interface instance, which lets class-based code such as drivers and monitors access the DUT's signals; it is passed in through config_db.",
382: "Simplify first: build the truth table or K-map and find the minimal sum of products (or product of sums), then look for shared terms and XOR patterns that reduce the count further, and consider which gate types are allowed (NAND-only or NOR-only designs need converting). Count the gates and literals in the final form, and show that removing any gate changes the function.",
383: "Read the code with the spec and look for the usual bugs: blocking assignments in always_ff, missing defaults causing latches, incomplete resets, width mismatches and sign issues, off-by-one counters, wrong FSM transitions, multiple drivers, race conditions and CDC without synchronisers. Simulate with a small testbench to reproduce the failure, trace it in the waveform back to the first wrong signal, then fix it and add an assertion or test so it stays fixed.",
384: "Count the register stages between each input and the output: every value assigned in an always @(posedge clk) block is one register, so the pipeline depth (latency) is the number of clocked assignments the data passes through. Combinational assigns add no depth. Watch for blocking assignments inside a clocked block, which can collapse what looks like two stages into one, and for feedback that makes the logic sequential rather than a pipeline.",
385: "A 2:1 MUX in CMOS can be built with two transmission gates: one passes A when S = 0, the other passes B when S = 1, with an inverter generating S-bar (6 transistors, or 8 with an output buffer). A static complementary version implements y = A S' + B S as an AND-OR-invert followed by an inverter, which restores the level and drives loads better.",
386: "An inverting summing amplifier: each input goes through its own resistor Ri into the inverting input, a feedback resistor Rf goes from the output to the inverting input, and the non-inverting input is grounded. Then Vout = -Rf (V1/R1 + V2/R2 + ...). If you swap the inputs (feedback to the non-inverting input), the feedback becomes positive, so the circuit no longer settles at a linear operating point: the output saturates at a rail and behaves like a comparator or latch.",
387: "Two PMOS in parallel from VDD to the output and two NMOS in series from the output to ground. The logic function does not change with different input delays: in steady state the output is always NAND of the inputs. But the timing does: the delay depends on which input switches (the NMOS nearer the output is faster because the lower node is precharged) and glitches can occur when the inputs change at different times.",
388: "PMOS from VDD to the output, NMOS from the output to ground, gates tied to the input. A low input turns the PMOS on (output high); a high input turns the NMOS on (output low). If VDD is removed (floating or 0), the output can no longer be driven high: for a low input it floats or follows leakage, and for a high input the NMOS still pulls it low. The inverter stops working and its inputs may back-power the supply through ESD diodes.",
389: "Each transistor has four terminals: gate, source, drain and body. For the PMOS (pull-up), the source and body connect to VDD, the gate to the input, and the drain to the output. For the NMOS (pull-down), the source and body connect to ground, the gate to the input, and the drain to the output. Tying the bodies this way keeps the junctions reverse biased.",
390: "PMOS A and PMOS B in parallel between VDD and the output; NMOS A and NMOS B in series between the output and ground. When both inputs are 1, both NMOS conduct and both PMOS are off, so the output is 0. If either input is 0, its PMOS pulls the output to 1 and the series NMOS path is open. The pull-down and pull-up networks are duals, so there is never a static path from VDD to ground.",
391: "A CMOS AND gate is a NAND gate followed by an inverter: two parallel PMOS and two series NMOS make the NAND, then a PMOS and NMOS pair inverts it, 6 transistors in total. There is no single-stage non-inverting CMOS AND gate, because static CMOS stages are naturally inverting.",
392: "Inverter: one PMOS to VDD and one NMOS to ground. NAND: parallel PMOS, series NMOS. NOR: series PMOS, parallel NMOS. AND and OR: a NAND or NOR followed by an inverter. XOR: a complementary implementation with 12 transistors, or transmission-gate versions with 6 to 8. General rule: series NMOS for AND, parallel for OR, and the PMOS network is the dual.",
393: "On a p-type substrate, the NMOS sits directly in the substrate with n+ source and drain diffusions and a p+ body tap to ground; the PMOS sits in an n-well with p+ source and drain diffusions and an n+ well tap to VDD. Both have a thin gate oxide under a polysilicon or metal gate, separated by shallow trench isolation, with contacts up to the metal layers. The well and substrate taps prevent latch-up.",
394: "A p-type substrate (body) with two heavily doped n+ regions (source and drain), a thin gate oxide over the channel region between them, and a polysilicon or metal gate on top, with spacers on the sides. A p+ body contact ties the substrate to ground, and shallow trench isolation separates it from neighbouring devices. When the gate voltage exceeds Vt, an inversion layer of electrons forms the channel between source and drain.",
395: "Plot Vout against Vin: Vout stays at VDD for low inputs, drops steeply around the switching threshold VM (about VDD/2 for a balanced inverter), and settles at 0 for high inputs. Mark VIL and VIH where the slope is -1, VOH and VOL at the rails, and the noise margins NMH = VOH - VIH and NML = VIL - VOL. A steep transition means high gain and good noise immunity.",
396: "Part of the output is fed back to the inverting input, so the op-amp drives its output in the direction that reduces the difference between its inputs. With very high open-loop gain, the inputs stay at almost the same voltage (the virtual short), and the closed-loop gain is set by the feedback network, for example -Rf/Rin, independent of the op-amp's gain. Negative feedback stabilises the gain and extends bandwidth, lowers distortion and output impedance, at the cost of gain.",
397: "Fan-out is how many inputs one output can drive while staying within logic levels. Compute it for both states: fan-out(low) = IOL / IIL and fan-out(high) = IOH / IIH, and take the smaller of the two. For example IOL = 16 mA, IIL = 1.6 mA gives 10; IOH = 0.4 mA, IIH = 40 uA gives 10, so the fan-out is 10.",
398: "Treat the gate as an equivalent inverter. With both inputs switching together, the two series PMOS act like one with half the strength (Kp/2 = Kn here) and the two parallel NMOS like one with double strength (2 Kn). Setting the currents equal in saturation: 2 Kn (VM - Vtn)^2 = Kn (VDD - VM - |Vtp|)^2, so VM = (VDD - |Vtp| + sqrt(2) Vtn) / (1 + sqrt(2)), and with Vtn = 2 |Vtp| that becomes VM = (VDD + (2 sqrt(2) - 1) |Vtp|) / (1 + sqrt(2)). If only one input switches, the pull-down is a single NMOS and VM shifts higher.",
399: "A ring oscillator is an odd number of inverters connected in a loop. There is no stable state, so a transition travels around the ring forever, giving a square wave with period 2 N td (N stages of delay td). It is used to measure gate delay and process speed on test chips, as a VCO (with current-starved stages) in PLLs, and as an on-chip clock or random-number source.",
400: "In bulk CMOS, the parasitic PNP (in the n-well) and NPN (in the substrate) bipolar transistors form a PNPN thyristor between VDD and ground. A current injection (from ESD, overshoot, radiation or a supply glitch) can turn both on, each feeding the other, and they latch into a low-resistance path that draws large current until the power is removed, possibly destroying the chip. Prevention: well and substrate taps close together, guard rings around IO, lower well resistance, spacing between NMOS and PMOS, and SOI processes.",
401: "The switching threshold VM is the input voltage at which Vout = Vin, the middle of the transfer curve where both transistors are in saturation. Equating their currents gives VM = (Vtn + r (VDD - |Vtp|)) / (1 + r), with r = sqrt(kp / kn). With equal strengths (PMOS about 2 to 3 times wider), VM is about VDD/2; a stronger PMOS raises it and a stronger NMOS lowers it.",
402: "NMOS is faster: electrons have about 2 to 3 times the mobility of holes, so an NMOS of the same size conducts more current and switches a load faster. That is why PMOS transistors are made wider to match, why NAND gates (series NMOS) are preferred over NOR gates (series PMOS), and why pull-down paths are the fast ones.",
403: "The stored voltage is V = Q / C = 10 uC / 1 uF = 10 V. If the cell must not lose more than its whole charge before the refresh after 12.5 ms, the maximum allowed leakage (discharge) current is I = Q / t = 10 uC / 12.5 ms = 0.8 mA; in practice you allow only a fraction of the charge to leak (for example 10% gives 80 uA), so that the sense amplifier still reads the right value.",
404: "SRAM uses a 6-transistor latch per bit: fast (around 1 ns access), no refresh, made in the standard logic process, but about 4 to 6 times larger per bit. DRAM uses a capacitor and one transistor: dense and cheap, but slower, with destructive reads, refresh, and a special process. Caches need low latency and must sit on the same die as the core, so SRAM is used; DRAM is used for main memory where capacity matters more.",
405: "A C program's memory has text (code), read-only data (constants and string literals), initialised data (.data), zero-initialised globals and statics (.bss), the heap (malloc, growing up) and the stack (locals and return addresses, growing down), plus memory-mapped IO in embedded systems. Sizes are platform dependent: char 1 byte, short 2, int usually 4, long 4 or 8, long long 8, float 4, double 8, pointers 4 or 8, and bool (C99 _Bool) usually 1 byte, not 1 bit. Structs add padding for alignment.",
406: "Take lw x5, 8(x6) followed by add x7, x5, x8. Fetch: the lw is read from instruction memory at PC. Decode: registers are read (x6) and the immediate 8 is extended; the add follows one cycle behind. Execute: the ALU computes x6 + 8. Memory: the data cache is read at that address. Write-back: the value goes into x5. The add needs x5 in its execute stage, one cycle too early, so the hazard unit inserts one bubble and forwarding passes the loaded value from MEM/WB to the ALU.",
407: "Five instructions are in flight at once, each in a different stage: fetch from instruction memory, decode and register read, execute in the ALU, memory access, and write-back to the register file. Pipeline registers between stages hold each instruction's data and control signals. After the pipeline fills, one instruction completes every cycle. Hazards are handled with forwarding paths, a load-use stall, and branch prediction or flushing.",
408: "Pipelining overlaps instruction execution in stages, raising throughput. Ideal speedup over a non-pipelined design is the number of stages k: n instructions take (k + n - 1) cycles instead of n k. The paradox is that pipelining makes each instruction slower (extra register delay per stage), yet the program runs faster, because more instructions finish per unit time. Real speedup is limited by unbalanced stages, register overhead and hazard stalls: throughput = 1 / (cycle time x CPI).",
409: "A microprocessor chip is mainly the CPU cores with caches, an interconnect, a memory controller and IO links. An SoC integrates a whole system: CPU clusters, GPU, DSPs and accelerators, memory controllers, on-chip SRAM, a DMA engine, peripherals (UART, SPI, I2C, USB, PCIe), security and boot blocks, clocks, reset and power management, all connected by an on-chip bus or network (AMBA AXI, AHB, APB or a NoC).",
410: "The driver writes command buffers into memory; the command streamer (front end) fetches and parses them, sets state and dispatches work. In a graphics pipeline, vertices are fetched and processed by vertex shaders, assembled into primitives, clipped and rasterised into fragments, the fragments are shaded by pixel shaders and textured, then depth and stencil tests and blending write the result to the framebuffer. Compute work is dispatched as thread groups across the shader cores (SIMT warps), with caches and memory controllers feeding them.",
411: "256 KB / 8 KB = 32 chips. Each 8 KB chip needs 13 address lines (2^13 = 8192), and the full 256 KB needs 18 address lines; the top 5 lines go to a 5-to-32 decoder that selects one chip.",
412: "SRAM is volatile: each bit is held by the feedback of two cross-coupled inverters, which needs power to keep running. When power is removed, the latch loses its state, and at power-up it settles to a random value. Unlike DRAM it needs no refresh while powered.",
413: "Larger caches lower the miss rate but raise hit latency, area and power. Higher associativity reduces conflict misses but costs hit time and energy. Larger lines exploit spatial locality but waste bandwidth and raise miss penalty. Write-back saves bandwidth versus write-through but adds complexity. Inclusive hierarchies simplify coherence but waste capacity; exclusive ones maximise capacity. Multi-level hierarchies balance fast small L1s with large L2s and L3s.",
414: "A watchdog timer is a hardware counter that resets or interrupts the system if software fails to refresh (kick) it within a timeout. Normal software periodically restarts the timer; if the code hangs, crashes or loops, the timer expires and the watchdog resets the chip, recovering from faults without human intervention. Windowed watchdogs also flag refreshes that come too early.",
415: "Fetch: on each clock, PC <= next_pc; the instruction memory output and PC + 4 go into the IF/ID register (flushed on a taken branch, held on a stall). Decode: split the instruction into opcode, rs, rt, rd and immediate fields, read the register file, sign-extend the immediate, generate the control signals, and write them all into the ID/EX register. The hazard unit compares ID's source registers with a load in EX and stalls (holding PC and IF/ID and zeroing the ID/EX control) when needed.",
416: "After scan insertion, the DFT tool writes a scan DEF file that describes the scan chains: each chain's start and stop points, the ordered list of scan flops, and which segments may be reordered (floating or ordered sections, partitions). The place-and-route tool reads it to reorder the chains based on placement, shortening scan wires, and then writes an updated chain order that ATPG must use.",
417: "A lockup latch is inserted between scan flops in different clock domains (or with skewed clocks, or opposite edges) on a scan chain. It holds the data for half a cycle, so a late-clocked receiving flop does not capture the new value already shifted out of the earlier flop, avoiding hold violations during shift. It is a level-sensitive latch transparent on the opposite phase of the launching clock.",
418: "The 20 W bulb. Rated at 120 V, R = V^2 / P gives 720 ohm for the 20 W bulb and 240 ohm for the 60 W bulb. In series they carry the same current, I = 120 / 960 = 0.125 A, so the power is I^2 R: 11.25 W in the 20 W bulb and 3.75 W in the 60 W bulb. The higher-resistance bulb gets more power and glows brighter.",
419: "Use XOR for the sum without carries and AND for the carries: while (b != 0) { c = (a & b) << 1; a = a ^ b; b = c; } return a;. Other options: a - (-b), or incrementing in a loop, but the bitwise version is what interviewers expect because it mirrors a hardware adder.",
420: "Yes, a port can be launched or captured by several clocks (for example in different modes, or a port feeding flops in two domains). Specify a delay relative to each clock with set_input_delay or set_output_delay using the -clock option, and -add_delay for the second and later ones so they do not overwrite the first, optionally with -clock_fall. Then use set_false_path or clock groups between clocks that can never interact.",
421: "Short-circuit (crowbar) current flows while a CMOS gate switches: during the input transition, when the input is between Vtn and VDD - |Vtp|, both the PMOS and the NMOS are on, giving a direct path from VDD to ground. It is larger with slow input slews and with fast outputs, and adds about 10 to 20% to dynamic power. Keeping input transitions sharp and balancing input and output slews reduces it.",
422: "Cut-off (VGS < Vt): ID is about 0 (only subthreshold leakage, exponential in VGS). Linear or triode (VDS < VGS - Vt): ID = k [(VGS - Vt) VDS - VDS^2 / 2], a voltage-controlled resistor for small VDS. Saturation (VDS >= VGS - Vt): ID = (k/2)(VGS - Vt)^2 (1 + lambda VDS). Here k = mu Cox W/L. Short-channel devices show velocity saturation, making ID closer to linear in VGS.",
423: "Describe your scope in one breath: the blocks or subsystems you verify, the methodology (UVM, formal, emulation), and your ownership (test plan, testbench architecture, VIP, coverage closure, regressions, sign-off). Then give one concrete example: the hardest bug you found, how you found it and its impact, plus numbers (coverage, bugs, tape-outs). Mention how you work with designers and architects.",
424: "Get the per-unit-length resistance and capacitance for the layer from the technology file or the extraction tables: R per um = sheet resistance / width (plus via resistance), C per um = area, fringe and coupling capacitance for that layer, width and spacing. Multiply by 1000 um: for example 0.1 ohm/um gives 100 ohm and 0.2 fF/um gives 200 fF. Model the wire as a distributed RC (several pi segments) and its Elmore delay is about 0.5 R C plus the driver resistance times the total capacitance.",
425: "Check the summary first: the number of sinks, maximum insertion delay (latency), global and local skew against targets, transition and capacitance violations, and the number and type of clock buffers. Then look at the worst skew pairs and the longest and shortest paths, clock gating cells, crossings between clock domains that need balancing, and the clock tree's power. Compare pre- and post-route numbers and make sure timing uses the propagated clock.",
426: "Use one analysis imp per transaction type, declared with `uvm_analysis_imp_decl(_a) and `uvm_analysis_imp_decl(_b), so the receiver has separate write_a() and write_b() methods. Alternatively use one analysis FIFO per stream and get from each in its own thread, or a common base transaction class with $cast to the specific type in a single write().",
427: "Store a multi-byte value and inspect its first byte: uint32_t x = 1; little_endian = (*(uint8_t *)&x == 1);. A union of an integer and a byte array works the same way. Most compilers also provide macros (such as __BYTE_ORDER__), and many CPUs have a status bit showing the configured endianness.",
428: "Power-gate idle blocks (header or footer switches, with isolation and retention for state), use high-Vt cells on non-critical paths, lower the supply in standby (retention voltage for SRAMs), use reverse body bias, stop clocks and put memories into sleep modes, and choose longer channel lengths where speed is not needed. At system level, shut down whole domains in deep sleep.",
429: "Clocks: create_clock -period with -name on the clock port, plus set_clock_uncertainty, set_clock_latency and set_clock_transition; create_generated_clock for divided clocks. Inputs and outputs: set_input_delay and set_output_delay relative to the clock, set_driving_cell (or set_drive) on inputs and set_load on outputs. Design rules: set_max_capacitance and set_max_transition on the design or ports (and set_max_fanout). Add exceptions with set_false_path and set_multicycle_path.",
430: "(P + Y)(P' + Y) = P P' + P Y + P' Y + Y Y = 0 + Y (P + P') + Y = Y. By the distributive law it is directly Y + P P' = Y.",
431: "Noise margins measure how much noise a logic level can tolerate. NMH = VOH(min) - VIH(min): how far a high output can drop and still be read as high. NML = VIL(max) - VOL(max): how far a low output can rise and still be read as low. VIL and VIH are where the transfer curve's slope is -1. Larger margins mean better immunity to supply noise, crosstalk and ground bounce.",
432: "Low-Vt cells switch faster (more drive current) but leak much more, exponentially more as Vt drops. High-Vt cells leak far less but are slower. Designs use multi-Vt optimisation: high-Vt (or standard-Vt) cells everywhere by default to save leakage, and low-Vt only on critical paths that need the speed, swapping cells after timing analysis.",
433: "RTL: lint, CDC and RDC, functional verification and coverage. Synthesis: timing, area and power reports, design rule violations, equivalence (RTL versus netlist), and DFT DRC. Floorplan and placement: congestion, utilisation, timing and power grid. CTS: skew, latency and transitions. Routing: DRC, opens and shorts, timing, signal integrity. Sign-off: STA in all corners and modes, IR drop and EM, DRC, LVS, antenna, density, equivalence on the final netlist, and gate-level simulation.",
434: "Noise (signal integrity) reports show crosstalk effects: delta delay (aggressors switching in the same or opposite direction speed up or slow down the victim, affecting setup and hold) and functional noise. Glitch reports show bumps induced on a quiet victim net by coupling from switching aggressors: the glitch height and width compared with the receiving cell's noise immunity, because a large enough glitch can flip a latch or flop. Fixes: spacing, shielding, upsizing victims and downsizing aggressors.",
435: "volatile tells the compiler that a variable may change outside the normal program flow (a hardware register, an ISR or another thread), so every read and write in the code must really access memory: the compiler cannot cache it in a register, remove repeated reads, or reorder those accesses. It does not make accesses atomic or add memory barriers.",
436: "If the source rises above the body (for an NMOS, VSB > 0), the body effect increases the threshold voltage, making the transistor weaker and slower, as in stacked transistors. If the body is at a higher voltage than the source (forward body bias for NMOS), the threshold drops, making it faster but leakier; if it exceeds about 0.6 V, the source-body junction becomes forward biased and conducts, which can trigger latch-up. That is why bodies are normally tied to ground (NMOS) and VDD (PMOS).",
437: "A linker script tells the linker how to place the program in the target's memory: it defines the memory regions (flash, RAM with their addresses and sizes), maps the output sections (.text, .rodata, .data, .bss, stack and heap, interrupt vectors) to those regions, sets the entry point, and defines symbols (such as the start and end of .bss) that the startup code uses to copy and clear data. It is essential for embedded and bare-metal code.",
438: "A multicycle path is a path that is allowed more than one clock cycle to propagate, because the design only samples its result every N cycles (for example data that is enabled every other cycle). You tell STA with set_multicycle_path N -setup, and usually set_multicycle_path N-1 -hold so the hold check moves back accordingly. The design must guarantee the multicycle behaviour, which should be verified.",
439: "One-hot encoding uses one flip-flop per state, with exactly one bit high at a time. Next-state and output logic become simple (often one gate per bit), decoding is fast, and it suits FPGAs with plenty of flops. It uses more flip-flops than binary encoding (N versus log2 N), and needs care to recover from illegal states.",
440: "For an ideal N-bit ADC with a full-scale sine input, SNR = 6.02 N + 1.76 dB. For 16 bits that is 6.02 x 16 + 1.76 = about 98.1 dB. Real converters are lower because of noise and distortion, which the effective number of bits (ENOB) captures.",
441: "RISC uses a small set of simple, fixed-length instructions that each take about one cycle, a load-store architecture, many registers and a hardwired control unit, which makes pipelining easy. CISC uses many complex, variable-length instructions with memory operands and multi-cycle execution, often microcoded, for denser code. Modern CISC chips translate instructions into RISC-like micro-ops, so the practical difference is mainly in decode complexity.",
442: "In a struct each member has its own storage, so the size is at least the sum of its members (plus padding for alignment), and all members are valid at once. In a union all members share the same storage, so the size is that of the largest member and only one member is meaningful at a time. Unions are used to save memory or to view the same bits as different types, such as for endianness checks.",
443: "Modelling builds a description that predicts behaviour, such as a SPICE model of a transistor, a timing model of a cell, or an architecture model of a CPU. Characterisation measures or simulates a real circuit across conditions to extract the numbers that go into a model, for example running SPICE on each standard cell across PVT corners, loads and slews to fill the Liberty timing and power tables. Characterisation feeds the models.",
444: "A combinational circuit's outputs depend only on its present inputs; it has no memory or clock (gates, multiplexers, adders, decoders). A sequential circuit's outputs depend on the present inputs and the stored state, kept in latches or flip-flops, usually updated by a clock (registers, counters, FSMs). Sequential circuits are combinational logic plus memory and feedback.",
445: "A RISC CPU fetches fixed-length instructions that are simple to decode, does arithmetic only on registers, and moves data with separate load and store instructions, so each instruction flows through a simple pipeline in about one cycle. A CISC CPU decodes variable-length instructions that may read memory, compute and write memory in one instruction, traditionally taking many cycles through microcode; modern CISC cores split them into micro-ops that execute like RISC instructions.",
446: "Dynamic power falls with lower voltage and fewer transitions, but lower voltage makes transistors slower, which tempts designers to lower the threshold voltage, and leakage rises exponentially as Vt drops. So reducing dynamic power through voltage scaling increases leakage, and fast low-Vt cells increase leakage too. The balance is set per design with multi-Vt cells, DVFS and power gating: low Vt on critical paths, high Vt elsewhere, and gating to remove leakage when idle.",
447: "Decap cells are on-chip capacitors between VDD and VSS placed in the standard cell rows. They supply local charge during sudden current demands, reducing dynamic IR drop and supply noise. Place them near high-activity logic (clock buffers, wide buses, register banks), near power-hungry macros and in empty space after placement. They cost area and add some leakage, so use them where dynamic IR analysis shows hotspots.",
448: "It is register indirect addressing: the operand's memory address is held in a register, and AX is loaded from the memory location that the register points to. Note that on the real 8086, only BX, BP, SI and DI can be used as address registers, so MOV AX, [CX] is not a valid 8086 instruction; MOV AX, [BX] is the valid form.",
449: "Turn each interface signal that carries information into a field: rand fields for the values you drive (address, data, operation type, length, delays between items), constraints for legal combinations and the scenario's distributions, and non-rand fields for responses the driver fills in. Register it with the factory and field macros or do_copy and do_compare, and add a convert2string for debug. Keep timing out of the item: the driver handles the handshake.",
450: "Top-level pin placement assigns each IO or block pin to a location on the die or block edge. It is decided by the package and board (bump and ball map, signal grouping), the location of the blocks that connect to each pin (to shorten routes and meet timing), signal type (high-speed pairs and clocks need special placement and shielding), power and ESD needs, and routing congestion. The flow: get the pin list and constraints, place the pins (often guided by a first trial placement), check timing and congestion, then iterate with block owners.",
451: "Read the netlists, libraries (.lib), LEFs and constraints, and check them (missing cells, constraint sanity). Set the die and core size from area and target utilisation, define rows and the IO or pin placement, then place the macros: near their related logic and IO, along edges, oriented to keep pins facing the core, with halos and channels for routing. Build the power grid (rings, straps, macro connections), add blockages and endcap and tap cells, then run a quick placement and timing and congestion checks to refine it.",
452: "lo = 0, hi = n - 1; while lo <= hi: mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2; if a[mid] == key return mid; else if a[mid] < key then lo = mid + 1 else hi = mid - 1; return not found. The array must be sorted. Time complexity is O(log n) because the range halves each step; space is O(1) for the iterative version.",
453: "void merge_sort(int *a, int *tmp, int lo, int hi) { if (hi - lo < 1) return; int mid = (lo + hi) / 2; merge_sort(a, tmp, lo, mid); merge_sort(a, tmp, mid + 1, hi); int i = lo, j = mid + 1, k = lo; while (i <= mid && j <= hi) tmp[k++] = (a[i] <= a[j]) ? a[i++] : a[j++]; while (i <= mid) tmp[k++] = a[i++]; while (j <= hi) tmp[k++] = a[j++]; for (k = lo; k <= hi; k++) a[k] = tmp[k]; }. Time O(n log n) in every case; space O(n) for the temporary array; it is stable.",
454: "AHB (AMBA High-performance Bus) is a pipelined bus: each transfer has an address phase and a data phase, and the address of the next transfer overlaps with the data of the current one. Key signals: HADDR, HTRANS (IDLE, BUSY, NONSEQ, SEQ), HWRITE, HSIZE, HBURST (single, incrementing and wrapping bursts of 4, 8 and 16), HWDATA and HRDATA, HREADY for wait states and HRESP for errors. A decoder selects the slave, and in multi-master systems an arbiter grants the bus. AHB-Lite is the single-master version.",
455: "AXI has five independent channels: write address (AW), write data (W), write response (B), read address (AR) and read data (R), each with its own valid and ready handshake. This allows reads and writes at the same time, multiple outstanding transactions, out-of-order completion using IDs, and burst transfers described only by the start address and length. AHB has one shared address and data pipeline, one outstanding transfer per master, in-order responses and separate arbitration, so AXI gives much higher throughput and better latency hiding.",
456: "Use set_clock_uncertainty with -from and -to clocks: set_clock_uncertainty -setup 0.1 -from [get_clocks clkA] -to [get_clocks clkB], and a separate -hold value. It models jitter and skew between two related clocks (synchronous but from different sources or PLL outputs), and overrides the single-clock uncertainty for paths between those clocks.",
457: "Go through the testbench in order: check that every component is registered with `uvm_component_utils and created with type_id::create (not new) in build_phase, that super.build_phase is called, the virtual interface is fetched from config_db with the right path and type, the driver and sequencer are connected in connect_phase, objections are raised and dropped around the sequence, and analysis ports are connected to the scoreboard. Then demonstrate a factory override in the test: set_type_override_by_type(base_seq::get_type(), err_seq::get_type()) before the environment is built.",
458: "UVM callbacks let users add behaviour to a component without modifying it: the component calls `uvm_do_callbacks at hook points, and a test registers a callback object (for example to inject errors in a driver). A mailbox is a FIFO for passing objects between threads, with put and get (blocking), try_put and try_get, and an optional size bound. A semaphore controls access to a shared resource with get(n) and put(n) of keys, so only one thread (or n) uses it at a time.",
459: "An interface bundles related signals (and can contain tasks, assertions and modports) so the DUT and testbench connect through one port. A clocking block inside it defines when signals are sampled and driven relative to a clock (with input and output skews), which avoids races between testbench and design. A program block runs testbench code in the reactive region, also to avoid races (less used with UVM). A virtual interface is a handle to an interface instance that class-based code (drivers, monitors) uses to reach the signals.",
}
